// Practice quiz: Multi-stage builds
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: containers-cicd/containers-and-compose/multi-stage-builds

::o1 runtime stage base::In a two-stage Spring Boot Dockerfile, which base suits the final stage that only runs the jar? {
~A JDK image such as eclipse-temurin\:25-jdk #Not quite. The JDK's compilers are needed to build, not to run.
=A JRE image such as eclipse-temurin\:25-jre #Correct. Running a jar needs only the Java runtime.
~A Node image #Not quite. Node cannot run a Java jar.
~nginx\:alpine #Not quite. nginx serves static files; it has no Java runtime.
}

::o1 start command::The final stage of a back-end Dockerfile must start the jar when the container runs. Which line does that? {
~RUN java -jar app.jar #Not quite. RUN executes only while the image is being built.
~COPY --from\=build app.jar #Not quite. COPY adds files; it starts nothing.
=ENTRYPOINT ["java", "-jar", "app.jar"] #Correct. ENTRYPOINT sets the command the container runs.
~FROM app.jar #Not quite. FROM names a base image or earlier stage.
}

::o2 front-end copy::A front-end Dockerfile builds with Vite in a stage named build. Which line belongs in the nginx stage? {
~COPY --from\=build /app /usr/share/nginx/html #Not quite. That ships node_modules and the source too.
~RUN npm run build #Not quite. The nginx image has no Node; building belongs in the Node stage.
=COPY --from\=build /app/dist /usr/share/nginx/html #Correct. Only the built files go to the folder nginx serves.
~COPY . /usr/share/nginx/html #Not quite. That copies your source folder, not the built output.
}

::o2 build command::Which command in the Node stage writes the production files that nginx will serve? {
~npm run dev #Not quite. It starts a development server and writes no dist folder.
~npm start #Not quite. It starts the app; it does not produce static files for nginx.
~nginx -s reload #Not quite. That reloads nginx; it builds nothing.
=npm run build #Correct. With Vite, it writes the built site into dist.
}

::o3 what COPY --from does::What does COPY --from\=build do in a multi-stage Dockerfile? {
~Copies files from your project folder #Not quite. Plain COPY reads the build context; --from reads a stage or image.
=Copies files from the earlier stage named build into the current stage #Correct. It reads from that stage instead of the build context.
~Runs the build stage again #Not quite. It only copies files that stage produced.
~Merges every layer of the build stage into the final image #Not quite. Only the named paths are copied.
}

::o3 why smaller::Why is a multi-stage back-end image usually smaller than a one-stage one? {
=The final stage keeps only its base and the files copied into it, so the JDK, caches and source stay behind #Correct. Stages that are not last do not ship.
~Docker compresses multi-stage images harder #Not quite. Size comes from what the final stage contains.
~The build stage is deleted from the source code #Not quite. It stays in the Dockerfile; its contents just do not ship.
~COPY --from removes files from the build stage #Not quite. It copies; it removes nothing.
}
